WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.153
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online TOEFL preparation.

2
00:00:05.453 --> 00:00:08.990
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:19.675
Unit four: Reading: Academic Passage Main Idea and Details. Today you will identify the main idea, details and organisation of a university-level text.

4
00:00:19.975 --> 00:00:22.557
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.570
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:27.417 --> 00:00:28.476
Main idea.

7
00:00:29.483 --> 00:00:32.388
Main idea means the central point of a passage.

8
00:00:32.983 --> 00:00:34.391
State the main idea.

9
00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:37.612
Your turn. Say: Main idea.

10
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:41.642
Main idea.

11
00:00:42.383 --> 00:00:43.675
Supporting detail.

12
00:00:44.683 --> 00:00:48.808
Supporting detail means a fact or example that explains the main idea.

13
00:00:49.417 --> 00:00:51.277
Find a supporting detail.

14
00:00:51.883 --> 00:00:54.698
Your turn. Say: Supporting detail.

15
00:00:57.533 --> 00:00:59.057
Supporting detail.

16
00:00:59.817 --> 00:01:00.829
Passage.

17
00:01:01.817 --> 00:01:03.851
Passage means a section of text.

18
00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:07.239
The academic passage has six paragraphs.

19
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.862
Your turn. Say: Passage.

20
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:14.527
Passage.

21
00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:16.517
Organisation.

22
00:01:17.517 --> 00:01:20.201
Organisation means how a text is arranged.

23
00:01:20.817 --> 00:01:22.456
Notice the organisation.

24
00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:25.795
Your turn. Say: Organisation.

25
00:01:28.633 --> 00:01:29.901
Organisation.

26
00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:31.883
Cause and effect.

27
00:01:32.883 --> 00:01:35.638
Cause and effect means a reason and its result.

28
00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.586
The passage explains cause and effect.

29
00:01:39.183 --> 00:01:42.393
Your turn. Say: Cause and effect.

30
00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:46.676
Cause and effect.

31
00:01:47.417 --> 00:01:48.383
Contrast.

32
00:01:49.383 --> 00:01:51.882
Contrast means a difference between things.

33
00:01:52.483 --> 00:01:54.541
The writer shows a contrast.

34
00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.895
Your turn. Say: Contrast.

35
00:02:00.733 --> 00:02:01.769
Contrast.

36
00:02:02.517 --> 00:02:03.529
Sequence.

37
00:02:04.517 --> 00:02:07.108
Sequence means a series of events in order.

38
00:02:07.717 --> 00:02:09.124
Follow the sequence.

39
00:02:09.717 --> 00:02:12.195
Your turn. Say: Sequence.

40
00:02:15.033 --> 00:02:16.104
Sequence.

41
00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:18.025
Not mentioned.

42
00:02:19.017 --> 00:02:21.237
Not mentioned means not in the text.

43
00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:23.780
The option is not mentioned.

44
00:02:24.383 --> 00:02:26.978
Your turn. Say: Not mentioned.

45
00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:30.974
Not mentioned.

46
00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:35.510
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:39.894
Visal, what do questions on an academic passage ask?

48
00:02:40.983 --> 00:02:51.554
They ask about the main idea, details, the purpose of a paragraph and the meaning of words. You must find the answer in the passage, not in your own knowledge.

49
00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.591
How can I find the main idea?

50
00:02:55.683 --> 00:03:05.023
Read the first and last sentences of each paragraph. Together they usually show the central point and the way the passage is organised.

51
00:03:06.117 --> 00:03:08.070
How do I answer a detail question?

52
00:03:09.183 --> 00:03:20.137
Find the keyword in the question, locate it in the passage and read the sentences around it. Wrong options often contain true facts that do not answer the question.

53
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.241
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:24.083 --> 00:03:29.322
Grammar focus: Strategy: skim for structure, read closely for details.

55
00:03:29.622 --> 00:03:30.786
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:31.483 --> 00:03:34.690
The first sentence often gives the main idea of the paragraph.

57
00:03:35.583 --> 00:03:39.487
A wrong option may be true in the passage but not answer the question.

58
00:03:40.433 --> 00:03:47.985
Skim for the structure, then read closely only where the question points. Eliminate options that are not mentioned or only partly true.

59
00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:52.679
Be careful! Don't say: I choose any option that is true.

60
00:03:54.633 --> 00:03:57.608
Say: I choose the option that answers.

61
00:03:57.908 --> 00:03:59.072
True is not enough.

62
00:03:59.867 --> 00:04:02.275
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:04:03.067 --> 00:04:04.590
Passage on Bees.

64
00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:08.097
The passage explains why honeybees are important to farms.

65
00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:13.758
The first paragraph gives the main idea: bees help plants produce fruit.

66
00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:19.262
Later paragraphs describe how pollination works and why bee numbers are falling.

67
00:04:20.267 --> 00:04:24.820
The last paragraph contrasts small farms with large industrial farms.

68
00:04:25.833 --> 00:04:28.843
A question asks for the cause of the falling numbers.

69
00:04:29.933 --> 00:04:34.028
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:34.883 --> 00:04:37.440
The main idea is often in the blank sentence.

71
00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.823
A, title only. B, first. C, last page.

72
00:04:45.367 --> 00:04:47.204
The answer is B: first.

73
00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:50.257
A wrong option may be blank.

74
00:04:50.557 --> 00:04:54.466
A, always false. B, a map. C, true but irrelevant.

75
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.696
The answer is C: true but irrelevant.

76
00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:03.919
What do bees help plants produce?

77
00:05:04.219 --> 00:05:07.339
A, fruit. B, stone. C, oil.

78
00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:13.120
The answer is A: fruit.

79
00:05:14.283 --> 00:05:16.608
Cause and effect shows a reason and blank.

80
00:05:16.908 --> 00:05:20.457
A, a map. B, a result. C, a title.

81
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.930
The answer is B: a result.

82
00:05:27.083 --> 00:05:29.512
The last paragraph contrasts blank.

83
00:05:29.812 --> 00:05:34.383
A, bees and ants. B, day and night. C, small and large farms.

84
00:05:37.933 --> 00:05:40.618
The answer is C: small and large farms.

85
00:05:41.833 --> 00:05:44.776
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:45.617 --> 00:05:47.906
How do you find the main idea of a passage?

87
00:05:56.717 --> 00:05:58.310
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:14.338
I skim the first and last sentences of each paragraph to understand the structure. Then I think about what the whole passage explains. For details, I find the keyword in the passage and read the sentences around it before I choose an answer.

89
00:06:14.638 --> 00:06:16.289
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:06:17.183 --> 00:06:23.806
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: skim for structure, read closely for details.

91
00:06:24.106 --> 00:06:28.523
Today's value is analytical thinking. Look for the pattern underneath the surface.

92
00:06:28.823 --> 00:06:31.185
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

93
00:06:31.485 --> 00:06:35.368
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

